**Data.** {{ n0(F.dataset.total_recipes) }} LLM recipes from {{ F.dataset.n_models }} models: {{ join_and(F.complete_names) }} complete{% if F.pending_core %}; {% for k in F.incomplete_models if F.models[k].tier == 'core' %}{{ F.models[k].name }} {{ F.models[k].status }}{% endfor %}{% endif %}. Proposal minimum for D1: {{ "met" if F.dataset.meets_proposal else pending("not yet met") }}.

1. **The lab's framework is reproduced.** Our pipeline matches Singh & Bagler's Fig. 4 in {{ R.random.sign_agreement }}/{{ R.random.n }} signs (random null), with Spearman ρ = {% for n in R.nulls %}{{ f(n.rho, 3) }} ({{ n.name|lower }}){{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} against the published Z. Korea's top contributor is {{ R.korea_top1 }}, as published. **[FINAL]**

2. **A cuisine's food-pairing sign depends on how many recipes you sample.** The own-pool null draws from the sample's own ingredients; small samples lose the rare, low-sharing ones, so the null scores higher and ΔNs falls (USA at {{ E.n_sub }} recipes: ΔNs = {{ f(E.usa.delta_ns_own_pool_n160) }} against its own pool, {{ f(E.usa.delta_ns_full_pool_n160) }} against the full pool). Cut to {{ E.matched_n }} recipes, all {{ E.contrasting_robust }}/{{ F.regions.n_contrasting }} contrasting cuisines stay contrasting, {{ E.uniform_robust }}/{{ F.regions.n_uniform }} uniform cuisines stay positive in ≥ 95% of samples, and {{ join_and(E.uniform_flipped) }} flip. The pre-registered sign-error rule fires on {{ pct(E.fpr) }} of genuine {{ E.n_sub }}-recipe samples, so LLM cuisines are judged against genuine samples of the same size. **[FINAL]**

3. **LLMs do not drift toward uniform food pairing; the errors are model-specific.** Against equal-size genuine samples, H1 (shift toward uniform) is supported for {{ V.h1eq_supported }}/{{ V.n }} models: {% for k, p in L.items() %}{{ p.name }} typical in {{ p.equal_n_typical }}/{{ p.regions }} regions, too uniform in {{ p.equal_n_too_uniform }}, too contrasting in {{ p.equal_n_too_contrasting }}{{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} Same verdict in every model with data: {% for c in F.consensus %}{{ c.region }} {{ c.verdict }} ({{ c.k }}/{{ c.k }}){{ ", " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} Sign agreement with the full cuisine is {{ rng(rs.sign_agreement) }} vs {{ f(E.genuine_sign_agreement) }} for genuine samples. **[{{ prov }}]**

4. **LLMs narrow cuisines rather than caricature them.** Signature ingredients are used about as often as in real recipes (median SAI {{ rng(rs.median_sai) }}; genuine 95% range {{ f(E.sai_band[0], 3) }}–{{ f(E.sai_band[1], 3) }}; H2b supported for {{ V.h2b_sai_supported }}/{{ V.n }} models), but each model uses only {{ pctr(rs.median_diversity_ratio) }} of the distinct ingredients of an equal-size genuine sample (significant in {{ V.h2b_div_supported }}/{{ V.n }}), and ingredient prevalence differs from the real cuisine by JSD {{ rng(rs.median_prevalence_jsd, 3) }} vs {{ f(E.ref.prevalence_jsd, 3) }} for genuine samples. **[{{ prov }}]**

5. **Category fingerprints are weakened.** LLM fingerprints correlate with the real ones at ρ = {{ rng(rs.median_fingerprint_spearman) }} vs {{ f(E.ref.fingerprint_spearman) }} for genuine resamples (H2c supported for {{ V.h2c_supported }}/{{ V.n }} models; regions below the resampling range: {% for k, p in L.items() %}{{ p.name }} {{ p.h2c_regions_below }}/{{ p.regions }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}). {% set DR = F.drivers %}{% if DR %}The flavor drivers (RQ2a) are also only partly recovered: median overlap of the top-5 χ% contributors with the real cuisine (Jaccard@5) {% for k in DR.order %}{{ DR.models[k].name }} {{ f(DR.models[k].median_jaccard5) }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} vs {{ f(DR.real.median_jaccard5) }} for genuine samples; for Korea, {{ join_and(DR.korea_match) }} put {{ DR.korea_real_top5[0] }} first as the real cuisine does{% if DR.korea_miss %}, {{ join_and(DR.korea_miss) }} not{% endif %}.{% else %}RQ2a: {{ pending("drivers") }}.{% endif %} **[{{ prov }}]**

6. **LLMs beat a copy-mutate model on composition, not on pairing strength.** Copy-mutate (Tuwani et al. 2019, ranked fitness; a baseline) reproduces the popularity curve (rank-frequency MAE {{ f(cm.freq_rank_mae, 3) }}) but not composition (JSD {{ f(cm.prevalence_jsd) }}), signatures (SAI {{ f(cm.sai) }}) or fingerprints (ρ {{ f(cm.fingerprint_spearman) }}), and gets the pairing sign right in {{ pct(cm.sign_match, 0) }} of regions. LLMs are closer to real on prevalence JSD in {{ V.cm_better.prevalence_jsd }}/{{ V.n }} models, fingerprint ρ {{ V.cm_better.fingerprint_spearman }}/{{ V.n }}, SAI {{ V.cm_better.abs_log_sai }}/{{ V.n }}, but on the food-pairing gap only {{ V.cm_better.abs_d_gap }}/{{ V.n }} ({{ join_and(F.cm_gap_models) }}). **[{{ prov }}]** (copy-mutate numbers: FINAL)

7. **A home-cook persona improves composition, not pairing.** Persona minus plain lowers prevalence JSD in {{ V.persona_jsd_better }}/{{ V.n }} models and raises fingerprint ρ only for {{ join_and(F.persona_rho_models) }}; the pairing gap improves in {{ V.persona_gap_better }}/{{ V.n }}. Proprietary vs open ({{ join_and(F.open_models) }}): {% for m, r in F.proprietary.items() %}{{ F.metric_names[m] }} {{ sf(r.effect_num, 3) }} (p_adj {{ pv(r.p_adj) }}){{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} Scale contrast: {{ "available" if F.scale else pending("needs the Qwen3.5-4B run") }}. **[{{ prov }}]**

